Hero Finder: the beacon advertises as "Hero Finder (<its ID>)", and nothing built carries Qorvo's name.

Qorvo's QANI advertises "<BoardName> (<DEVICEADDR[0] in hex>)", such as "DWM3001CDK (86E7C53A)". With this patch the
board name is "Hero Finder", so a board advertises "Hero Finder (86E7C53A)". The eight hex digits stay: they are the
beacon's ID, the only one a phone can read without connecting (QANI takes one connection at a time, and iOS hides the
Bluetooth address), so the phone, Influence's API and a printed label all name a beacon by them. The application's
name, which the firmware logs, loses "Qorvo" too: Qorvo's licence (LicenseRef-QORVO-2, clause 5) lets nothing built
from its source carry Qorvo's name. The file is LicenseRef-QORVO-2, and its notice is untouched.

Apply from the SDK source's root: patch -p1 < 0001-hero-finder-name.patch (qani/build does).

--- a/Projects/FreeRTOS/QANI/DWM3001CDK/ProjectDefinition/ProjectName.c
+++ b/Projects/FreeRTOS/QANI/DWM3001CDK/ProjectDefinition/ProjectName.c
@@ -10,7 +10,7 @@
  *
  */

-const char ProjectName[] = "DWM3001CDK - QANI - FreeRTOS";
-const char BoardName[] = "DWM3001CDK";
+const char ProjectName[] = "Hero Finder - DWM3001CDK - FreeRTOS";
+const char BoardName[] = "Hero Finder";
 const char OsName[] = "FreeRTOS";
-const char ApplicationName[] = "Qorvo Nearby Interaction";
+const char ApplicationName[] = "Hero Finder";
